From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 13:55:18 -0400
Subject: give the window Arc's proportions

Three changes, all aimed at the gap recorded in docs/UI-SPEC.md between
Chromium's vertical tab strip and Arc's window chrome.

SteddingBrowserViewLayout rounds the contents corner nearest the tab strip,
so the window background reads as a mat around the page rather than the page
running into the corner. Upstream only ever rounds that area in glass mode
(macOS 26 plus a disabled-by-default feature), and rounds the bottom corner
rather than the top; this subclasses BrowserViewTabbedLayoutImpl and adjusts
visuals after layout rather than editing that file, which takes 137 commits a
year. Where upstream has already chosen radii -- split view, and the shadow box
it draws under a side panel -- those are kept, because they come with an inset
that has already pulled the contents off the window edge.

The tab strip gets Arc's proportions: 352 DIP wide by default instead of 240,
44 DIP tall rows instead of 30, and a 10 DIP pill instead of 8. Width stays
resizable and pref-backed exactly as before -- only the default moves -- and
the maximum goes to 480 so the new default is not pinned against it.

Favicons stay at 16 DIP. The spec says 18, but gfx::kFaviconSize is global and
the icon would not scale with the slot, so the difference is not worth the
blast radius.

Why: the square, edge-to-edge contents and the narrow, tightly-packed tab rows
are the two things that most make the window read as Chromium rather than Arc.
Removable when: upstream's glass frame ships enabled by default and rounds the
contents on all platforms, and the vertical tab strip's default metrics are
themable without a patch.
